WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.160
- [Instructor] My book is 58 pages.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.990
I have already read 13 pages.

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:09.680
I plan to read five pages each day

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.220
until I finish the book.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.990
Which equation could I use to find out

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:20.990
how many days, d, it will take
to finish reading the book?

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.580
So pause this video and see
if you can figure that out.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:25.630
Okay, so let's first think

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.210
about the important
information they're giving us.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:32.160
They're giving us the number
of pages that are in the book.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.200
They're giving us how
much we've already read.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.000
We've already read 13 pages.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.110
And they also tell us
how much we plan to read.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.280
I plan to read five pages each day

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.980
until the book is finished.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.620
And then we want an
equation that could use

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:48.990
to find how many days.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.280
And d is how we're going to
represent the number of days,

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.790
let me do it in the same color,

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:54.650
it'll take to finish reading the book.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:58.450
All right, so if we knew how
many pages we have to read

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.210
and we were divided by the fact

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:02.970
that we're reading five pages per days,

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.990
well, then that would give
us the number of days.

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:08.080
So how many pages do we have
left to read in the book?

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.700
Well, the book is 58 pages.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:15.310
We've already read 13, so we
could subtract out 13 of those.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.250
So this expression right over here,

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:21.220
this is how many pages
we have left in the book.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:22.053
Let me write that down.

00:01:22.053 --> 00:01:26.210
How many pages left.

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.310
And if we wanna figure out
how many days it's going

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:31.530
to take to read these,

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:34.800
we would divide this by how
many pages we read each day.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.820
And we see that we read
five pages each day.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:42.820
So if we divide this by five,
then this whole expression,

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.790
let me do it in this color,

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.820
this whole expression is going to tell us

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:51.120
how many days left.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.600
How many days left.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.670
And so this is going to be equal to d

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.640
'cause d is the number of days left

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.340
to finish reading the book.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:01.840
Now, if we look at these choices,

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.010
that's exactly what we have
right over here in choice C.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:08.060
We can make sure that these
others don't make sense.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.390
58 plus 13, no, that would be a situation

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.240
where we're reading 13 pages more

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:14.750
than the number of pages
that are in the book.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:15.740
That doesn't make sense.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.920
And that's happening for
both choice A and choice B.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:22.150
It would also not make
sense to multiply by five

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.470
because it's not going to
take you five times more

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.600
than the number of pages to read the book.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:28.790
You're going to divide by five.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:31.800
You're reading five pages every day.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:32.823
And we are done.

